General elections were held in [[Panama]] on Sunday, 2 May 2004, electing both a new [[List of Presidents of Panama|President of the Republic]] and a new [[Legislative Assembly of Panama|Legislative Assembly]].

==Results==
===President===
For the second consecutive election, Martín Torrijos, son of former military ruler [[Omar Torrijos]], was named the candidate of the [[Democratic Revolutionary Party]] (PRD); in 1999, he had lost to [[Mireya Moscoso]]. Torrijos ran on a platform of strengthening democracy and negotiating a free trade agreement with the US, and was supported by popular musician and politician [[Rubén Blades]];<ref name=E /> Torrijos later made Blades the nation's tourism minister.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-192683.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160312141157/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-192683.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=March 12, 2016 |title=Nicky Hilton Weds One Hilton ... |date=August 16, 2004 |work=The Washington Post|via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=September 18, 2012}}</ref> Torrijos' primary rival was [[Guillermo Endara]], who had served as president from 1990 to 1994. Endara ran as the candidate of the [[Solidarity Party (Panama)|Solidarity Party]], on a platform of reducing crime and government corruption.<ref name=AP>{{cite web |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-94049662.html |title=Guillermo Endara, former president of Panama, fights against corruption, crime |author=Mark Stevenson |date=May 2, 2004 |work=Associated Press |archive-date=September 23, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140923091102/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P1-94049662.html |via=HighBeam Research |url-access=subscription |url-status=usurped |access-date=August 31, 2012}}</ref> Endara and the other candidates also ran a series of negative ads highlighting the PRD's connections with former military ruler [[Manuel Noriega]].<ref name=WP52>{{cite web |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-168124.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160414115843/https://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-168124.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=April 14, 2016 |title=General's Son Leads in Panama; Running as a Pro-Capitalist Nationalist, Torrijos Emerges as Favorite in Polls |author=Mary Jordan |date=May 2, 2004 |work=The Washington Post |via=HighBeam Research |url-access=subscription |access-date=September 18, 2012}}</ref> Endara finished second in the race, receiving 31% of the vote to Torrijos' 47%.<ref name=E>{{cite web |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-116378324.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140928140916/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1G1-116378324.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=September 28, 2014 |title=Not his father's son? Panama's new president |date=May 8, 2004 |work=The Economist |via=HighBeam Research |url-access=subscription |location=US |access-date=August 31, 2012}}</ref>

Torrijos assumed office on 1 September 2004. Voters also elected his two vice-presidents, who run on party tickets in conjunction with the presidential candidates.

